"Internal Error ()\nciteturn2view0 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www2.math.upenn.edu/~pemantle/papers/crit-ising.pdf\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1\nL0: Failed to fetch https://www2.math.upenn.edu/~pemantle/papers/crit-ising.pdf: (403) Forbidden\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[2404.11564] Non-linear conductances of Galton-Watson trees and application to the (near) critical random cluster model (https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.11564)\nciteturn2view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.11564\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 165\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Mathematics > Probability\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 17 Apr 2024 (cite5†v1 ), last revised 22 Nov 2025 (this version, v2)]\nL9: # Title:Non-linear conductances of Galton-Watson trees and application to the (near) critical random cluster model\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite6†Irene Ayuso Ventura , cite7†Quentin Berger L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled Non-linear conductances of Galton-Watson trees and application to the (near) critical random cluster model, by Irene Ayuso Ventura and Quentin Berger\nL14: \nL15: cite8†View PDF cite9†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:In this article, we study concave recursions on trees, which appear widely in information theory through algorithms such as belief propagation, and in statistical mechanics through models on tree-like graphs, including the Ising model, percolation, and more generally, the random cluster model. These tree recursions can, in fact, be compared with non-linear conductances, or $p$-conductances, between the root and the leaves of the tree.\nL17: In this article, we estimate the $p$-conductances of $T_n$, a supercritical Galton--Watson tree of depth $n$, for any $p>1$, for a quenched realization of $T_n$. In particular, we find the sharp asymptotic behavior when $n$ goes to infinity, which depends on whether the offspring distribution admits a finite moment of order $q$, where $q=\\frac{p}{p-1}$ is the conjugate exponent of $p$.\nL18: We then apply our results to the random cluster model on $T_n$ (with cluster weight parameter in $(0,2]$ and wired boundary condition) providing sharp estimates on the probability that the root is connected to the leaves.\nL19: As an example, for the Ising model on $T_n$ with plus boundary conditions on the leaves, we find that, at criticality, the quenched magnetization of the root decays like: (i) $n^{-1/2}$ times an explicit tree-dependent constant if the offspring distribution admits a finite third moment; (ii) $n^{-1/(\\alpha-1)}$ if the offspring distribution has a heavy tail with exponent $\\alpha \\in (1,3)$.\nL20: Comments:  | Revised version. 38 pages, 1 figure, comments welcome\nL21: Subjects:  | Probability (math.PR); Mathematical Physics (math-ph)\nL22: MSC (Mathematical Subject Classification) classes:  | 60K35, 31C45, 82B27\nL23: Cite as:  | cite10†arXiv:2404.11564 [math.PR]\nL24:    | (or cite11†arXiv:2404.11564v2 [math.PR] for this version)\nL25:    | cite12†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2404.11564†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL26: ## Submission history\nL27: \nL28: From: Quentin Berger [cite13†view email ]\nL29: cite5†[v1] Wed, 17 Apr 2024 17:06:34 UTC (69 KB)\nL30: [v2] Sat, 22 Nov 2025 14:21:52 UTC (71 KB)\nL31: \nL32: Full-text links:\nL33: \nL34: ## Access Paper:\nL35: \nL36: View a PDF of the paper titled Non-linear conductances of Galton-Watson trees and application to the (near) critical random cluster model, by Irene Ayuso Ventura and Quentin Berger\nL37: \nL38:   * cite8†View PDF L39:   * cite9†HTML (experimental) L40:   * cite14†TeX Sourc